  "text": "olving the length of \\( EF \\) in terms of the side length \\( s \\) of a regular pentagon can be approached through analyzing the geometric relationships within the pentagon and using principles similar to those seen in the isosceles triangle in the original problem.\n\nIn a regular pentagon, the interior angles are all \\( 108^\\circ \\) (since \\( 180^\\circ - 72^\\circ = 108^\\circ \\)), and the central angles subtended by the sides are all \\( 72^\\circ \\). Given this configuration, we can see that triangle \\( AEF \\) is isosceles with \\( \\angle FAE = 30^\\circ \\), making \\( \\angle EAF = 15^\\circ \\) because the sum of the angles in a triangle is \\( 180^\\circ \\).\n\nTo find \\( EF \\), we need to utilize the properties of the regular pentagon and the trigonometric relationships within triangle \\( AEF \\).\n\n1. **Determine the Length of \\( AE \\)**: Note that in a regular pentagon, the side length \\( s \\) is equal to the line segment connecting two vertices that subtend an angle of \\( 108^\\circ \\) in the pentagon. However, to find the length \\( AE \\), we look at the line segment connecting vertex \\( A \\) to any vertex bisected by the line of symmetry. Given the symmetry and the 30-degree angle, \\( AE \\) would be \\( s \\).\n\n2. **Express \\( EF \\) Using Trigonometry**: In triangle \\( AEF \\), we have \\( \\angle AEF = 36^\\circ \\) (half of the central angle) and \\( AE = s \\). We can use the Law of Sines to find \\( EF \\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin(\\angle EAF)}{EF} = \\frac{\\sin(\\angle AEF)}{AE}\n\\]\n\nPlugging in the values:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin(15^\\circ)}{EF} = \\frac{\\sin(36^\\circ)}{s}\n\\]\n\n3. **Calculate \\( EF \\)**: To isolate \\( EF \\), we rearrange the equation:\n\n\\[\nEF = s \\cdot \\sec(\\angle AEF) = s \\cdot \\sec(36^\\circ)\n\\]\n\nKnowing \\( \\sec(36^\\circ) \\) can be calculated using trigonometric identities or tables. In an isosceles triangle with a \\( 30^\\circ \\) angle, the secant of a \\( 36^\\circ \\) angle is not a standard value, but we can approximate it knowing that:\n\n\\[\n\\sec(36^\\circ) \\approx \\frac{1}{\\cos(36^\\circ)} \\approx \\frac{1}{0.8090}\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\nEF \\approx s \\cdot \\frac{1}{0.8090} \\approx 1.23 \\cdot s\n\\]\n\nHowever, as stated in the original suggestion, the correct mathematical tool here might be the cosine rule rather than the length of an extended leg through the central angle. The cosine rule in triangle \\( AEF \\) would give a different perspective leading to:\n\n\\[\nEF^2 = AE^2 + AF^2 - 2(AE)(AF)\\cos(30^\\circ)\n\\]\n\nGiven \\( AE = s \\) and considering \\( AF \\) as \\( s \\) due to the symmetry of the regular pentagon, the expression simplifies to find \\( EF \\), but since \\( AF \\) would be \\( s \\) also, the term \\( 2(s)^2\\cos(30^\\circ) \\) effectively reduces the computation. The cosine of \\( 30^\\circ \\) is \\( \\sqrt{3}/2 \\), and so:\n\n\\[\nEF = \\sqrt{s^2 + s^2 - 2(s)(s)(\\sqrt{3}/2)} = \\sqrt{2s^2 - s^2\\sqrt{3}} = s\\sqrt{2 - \\sqrt{3}}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the length of segment \\( EF \\) is \\( s\\sqrt{2 - \\sqrt{3}} \\), where \\( s \\) is the side length of the regular pentagon. This solution leverages the properties of trigonometry, especially the values of sine, cosine, and secant, in conjunction with the geometry of a regular pentagon.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sqrt{2 - \\sqrt{3}}s}\n\\]",
